N1-N4 and the SAIRNfreedom deletes -- criteria 2026-10-14.1

N1. a recorded EVV clock time cannot be replaced silently
  ok       N1a. CONTROL -- a FIRST clock-in on a visit with none recorded is accepted. Without this arm the fix could be "refuse every clock write" and every arm below would still pass
  ok       N1b. THE ARM THE WHOLE SECTION IS FOR -- a DIFFERENT clock_in_at over a recorded one does NOT replace it: the original is still on the row and the submission is appended to the correction trail. This module's own words: "a state audit that finds an edited time with no trail cannot tell a typo correction from a fabrication"
  ok       N1c. CONTROL -- an IDENTICAL re-send is still accepted. sairnsenior queues clock events offline and replays them UNCHANGED, so the same visit legitimately arrives twice; refusing that would break the replay path this module is written around
  ok       N1d. CONTROL -- the OTHER half of the visit still writes. The guard is per field, so a clock-out after a recorded clock-in is the normal case and is not collateral damage
  ok       N1e. ...and clock_out_at is covered too, not just clock_in_at. A rule proven on one of two fields is a rule that covers one of two fields

N2. sen-auth setup cannot leave the licence with no Owner
  ok       N2a. the SOLE active Owner cannot be re-roled to caregiver by setup (409 LAST_ADMIN). set_active has been guarded against the same end state since the lifecycle module was written; setup reached it through the ROLE instead of the active flag
  ok       N2b. CONTROL -- with a SECOND active Owner the same call succeeds. Without this arm the fix could be "never change an owner's role" and N2a would still pass
  ok       N2c. CONTROL -- provisioning a NEW caregiver while one Owner exists is untouched. The guard is about the target being the last Owner, not about the roster size
  ok       N2d. CONTROL -- re-running setup on the sole Owner AS an owner (a PIN reset) still works. A guard that blocked that would lock the one person who can fix anything out of their own password

N3. a requirement that excluded this position is disclosed
  ok       N3a. a requirement that returned a clean `false` for this position is listed in `excluded_by_position` with the reason matchAudience gave. It used to vanish: `unmapped_requirements` is built only from STRUCTURALLY unmappable rows, so a row WITH a position list never appeared there no matter who it excluded
  ok       N3b. ...and the caveat says the excluded hours are NOT in `required_annual_hours`, because under-inclusion that nobody can see is indistinguishable from the requirement not existing -- which was the author's own self-flag on this point
  ok       N3c. CONTROL -- the key is ABSENT when nothing was excluded, not an empty array on every finding

N4. no comment claims an arm that does not exist
  FAIL     N4a. neither bare "driven" claim survives in api/_lib/compliance-rules.js. They most likely meant DRIVEN BY HAND, which is a real activity -- the defect is that a hand-drive leaves no artefact, so six weeks later the comment is the only evidence and it reads like a reference to a committed arm
           the leap-day normalisation
  ok       N4b. ...and the replacement CITES THE SUITE THAT NOW DRIVES THEM, and that suite is really on disk. A comment claiming an arm is only worth anything if the named file exists -- which is the b13 N4 defect, so this arm checks both halves rather than the wording alone
  ok       N4c. an impossible hire date yields NO window and a named window_error, instead of an impossible window start printed on a regulated finding. REFUSED, NOT REPAIRED: normalising 2024-02-30 would be this code choosing which date somebody was hired on
  ok       N4d. CONTROL -- a REAL leap-day hire and a real end-of-year hire still compute a window (2024-02-29 -> 2026-02-28 by the day-decrement normalisation, 2024-12-31 -> 2025-12-31). The refusal is for dates that are not dates; a date that merely has no counterpart in the target year is still normalised, and that distinction is the whole of it

F6. sfDeleteEvent reported a removal that did not happen
  ok       F6a. sfDeleteSession CLEARS the event's sessionId. It used to remove the session and leave the pointer, and the UI offers Remove on every session row -- so the stale pointer was one click away
  ok       F6b. ...and deleting that event does NOT announce "Event and its bingo session removed", because no session was removed. The toast used to be chosen on the POINTER being truthy and never on whether the filter matched -- affirmative false success, which is worse than silence because it tells the operator a thing happened. Said: "Event removed"
  ok       F6c. CONTROL -- when the session IS there, it is removed and the original message is still the one shown. The fix is to report the OUTCOME, not to stop reporting
  ok       F6e. an event whose sessionId points at a session that DOES NOT EXIST does not announce a session removal, and the unrelated session survives. This is the state every row written before today is in, so it is the arm that matters after a deploy. Said: "Event removed. Its bingo session was already gone, so nothing else was"
  ok       F6d. CONTROL -- an event with NO session says "Event removed" and leaves the unrelated session alone. Three outcomes, three messages

